# Python Port Plan — Step 04 · The API Client

## Purpose

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/04_api_client` to Python, producing
`week1_baseline/python/04_api_client` that is behaviourally equivalent:
same `Client#call` shape (POST the built payload, retry transient
failures/retryable status codes with exponential backoff, raise `ApiError`
on final failure, return the parsed JSON body), stdlib-only, no new
third-party dependencies — matching the Ruby README's explicit "no gems"
design intent for this step.

This is the fifth entry in the `docs/plans/python_port/<step>` series (see
[`context.md`](context.md) for the index). `03_prompt_builder` is the
direct predecessor (not yet ported at the time of this writing — see its
plan at [`03_prompt_builder`](03_prompt_builder)); this step adds
`Boukensha::Client` on top of it. One HTTP POST, one response — still no
tool-calling loop (that's `05_agent_loop`).

Confirmed via `diff` against `03_prompt_builder`: `backends/*.rb`,
`prompt_builder.rb`, `registry.rb`, `tool.rb`, `message.rb`, `context.rb`
are **byte-identical**. (The Ruby README's "New/Updated Files" tables claim
`backends/base.rb` and `backends/*.rb` changed in this step — they did not;
see the known-inconsistency note below.) `tasks/base.rb` gets one real
behavioural fix; `config.rb` gets one line that looks like a fix but is
actually a new bug; `errors.rb` adds `ApiError`.

